{
  "analysis": "The reviewer names several Monopoly Go mechanics in sequence. First, they describe having previously received rewards for inviting friends and getting them to join via Facebook, and that this reward has now stopped ('Suddenly I get no rewards for inviting friends and getting them to join facebook'). This names the recruitment-for-reward mechanic itself (Social Pyramid Scheme), even though the reviewer is complaining the payout broke; the underlying design of rewarding friend invitations is what is being described. Second, the reviewer describes accumulating over 500 dice (an in-game currency used for board moves) and then 'playing max bet' on a chance-based event, hoping to trigger Shutdowns or Bank Heists, but getting none. This is a wager of an in-game currency (dice, whether earned or purchased) on an outcome governed by chance, which is a gambling mechanic per the codebook's boundary language allowing wagers of 'paid or earned intermediate currency,' and the unpredictable delivery of the special event outcomes (Shutdowns/Heists never landing) is also a variable-reward delivery schedule. Third, the reviewer complains that stickers are duplicates 'all the way,' which describes a randomized collection-item pool yielding repeated, unwanted drops rather than new items needed to complete sticker sets, implicating both the collection-completion compulsion and the reward-delivery mechanism. Finally, the reviewer describes being locked out of the game entirely by a persistent internet connection error across three different wifi networks. This is explicitly attributed to a technical fault (an 'internet connection error') with no stated in-game penalty tied to a design rule (e.g., no forfeited streak or progress described), so per R11 this does not support any dark-pattern label on its own.",
