{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes a solitaire-style game where progression through numbered levels depends on an in-game economy. Two things are being reported together: first, a general statement that playing without spending real money is unrewarding, using the explicit contrast phrase 'without spending actual money'; second, a concrete illustration of that unrewarding economy — at level 100 the entry cost is 2200 (game currency), and even a win barely returns that amount, meaning the free/earned loop cannot sustain play and pushes the player toward spending. This is the Narrative Obligation shape of Pay to Progress: currency earned in-game trickles back at a rate that barely covers the cost of continuing, so payment becomes the practical way to keep progressing. The 'rng is so obviously against you' line is a bare, mechanism-free accusation of bad luck/unfairness — it doesn't describe a concealed or manipulated outcome-determination system (no fixed ratio, no rigged deal, no hidden schedule named), so per the Illusion of Control boundary rule this reads as an unlucky-run complaint rather than an instance of that pattern, and gets no label. 'The rewards need a big revamp' is an evaluative complaint about reward value, not a description of a delivery mechanism (variable rewards, gacha, complexity), so it does not support Reward Mania either. No currency name, social mechanic, ad, timer, or collection element is described, so no other labels apply.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "it's not rewarding playing the game without spending actual money.. at around level 100 it costs 2200 but if you win the level you'll barely make that back",
      "rule_applied": "vs Free alternative boundary rule / Narrative Obligation indicator",
      "rationale": "The review explicitly contrasts playing 'without spending actual money' as unrewarding, then illustrates it: the level's currency cost (2200) is barely recovered even by winning, so the earned/free loop cannot sustain play and payment becomes the practical route to progress."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
